# a float literal is the 64 bits of the double nearest to what it says:
# digits, a dot and digits, with an exponent or not and a "-" or not. The
# bits are those that float of Python 3 gives for the same text: a value
# between two doubles goes to the nearer, a tie to the one whose last
# bit is 0, one below the least double to a zero, and digits before the
# dot may be more than 64 bits hold. It stands wherever a number does:
# as a value, as a const, in an expression and after "as" of nothing.
use "lib/std/io.aiw"
fn show x
	call putn x
	call print "\n"
end
call show 1.5
call show 0.5
call show 0.1
call show -0.0
call show 0.0
call show 3.141592653589793
call show 2.5e-3
call show 2.5E3
call show 1.0e+2
call show 1.7976931348623157e308
call show 4.9e-324
call show 2.4e-324
call show 0.30000000000000004
call show 123456789012345678901234567890.5
call show 9007199254740993.0
call show -1.25
call show 000.250
call show 18446744073709551616.0
call show 1.0e-400
const half 0.5
fadd 1.5 half as x
ftoi x as i
call show i
y=2.5
fmul y 4.0 as z
ftoi z as i
call show i
flt 0.1 0.2 as c
call show c
# a number that is no float literal is the number it was
call show 15
call show -15
call show 0x10
